[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Graph Representations: Adjacency Matrix and Adjacency List"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Graph Representations"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Graphs are fundamental data structures used to model relationships between objects. Representing graphs efficiently is crucial for various algorithms and applications. Two common methods for representing graphs are the Adjacency Matrix and the Adjacency List."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Adjacency Matrix"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"An Adjacency Matrix is a 2D array (matrix) where rows and columns represent vertices of the graph. The entry at matrix[i][j] indicates whether there is an edge from vertex i to vertex j. For an unweighted graph, the entry is typically 1 if an edge exists and 0 otherwise. For a weighted graph, the entry represents the weight of the edge."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Represents a graph as a square matrix of size V x V, where V is the number of vertices."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"matrix[i][j] = 1 if there is an edge from vertex i to vertex j; otherwise, matrix[i][j] = 0 (for unweighted graphs)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"For weighted graphs, matrix[i][j] stores the weight of the edge from vertex i to vertex j."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Adjacency Matrix for an unweighted graph in C++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

int main() {
  int vertices = 4;
  vector<vector<int>> adjMatrix(vertices, vector<int>(vertices, 0));

  // Add edges (example)
  adjMatrix[0][1] = 1; // Edge from vertex 0 to vertex 1
  adjMatrix[1][2] = 1; // Edge from vertex 1 to vertex 2
  adjMatrix[2][0] = 1; // Edge from vertex 2 to vertex 0
  adjMatrix[3][2] = 1; // Edge from vertex 3 to vertex 2

  // Print the Adjacency Matrix
  for (int i = 0; i < vertices; ++i) {
    for (int j = 0; j < vertices; ++j) {
      cout << adjMatrix[i][j] << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
  }

  return 0;
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Adjacency List"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"An Adjacency List represents a graph as an array of lists. Each element of the array represents a vertex, and the corresponding list contains the vertices adjacent to that vertex. This representation is more space-efficient for sparse graphs (graphs with fewer edges)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Represents a graph as an array (or list) of lists."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Each index of the array represents a vertex."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The list at each index contains the vertices adjacent to that vertex (neighbors)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"For weighted graphs, the list can store pairs of (neighbor, weight)."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Adjacency List for an unweighted graph in C++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

int main() {
  int vertices = 4;
  vector<vector<int>> adjList(vertices);

  // Add edges (example)
  adjList[0].push_back(1); // Edge from vertex 0 to vertex 1
  adjList[1].push_back(2); // Edge from vertex 1 to vertex 2
  adjList[2].push_back(0); // Edge from vertex 2 to vertex 0
  adjList[3].push_back(2); // Edge from vertex 3 to vertex 2

  // Print the Adjacency List
  for (int i = 0; i < vertices; ++i) {
    cout << "Adjacency list of vertex " << i << ": ";
    for (int j = 0; j < adjList[i].size(); ++j) {
      cout << adjList[i][j] << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
  }

  return 0;
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparison: Adjacency Matrix vs. Adjacency List"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Space Complexity: Adjacency Matrix: O(V^2), Adjacency List: O(V + E), where V is the number of vertices and E is the number of edges."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Time Complexity to check edge existence: Adjacency Matrix: O(1), Adjacency List: O(V) in the worst case."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Adjacency Matrix is preferred for dense graphs (many edges), while Adjacency List is preferred for sparse graphs (few edges)."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Adjacency Matrix is simpler to implement, but Adjacency List is more memory-efficient for sparse graphs."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right graph representation depends on the specific application and the characteristics of the graph. Adjacency Matrices offer fast edge lookups but consume more memory, while Adjacency Lists are more space-efficient for sparse graphs but have slower edge lookups. Understanding the trade-offs between these representations is essential for efficient graph algorithm design."}]
